---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site';
---
<Layout
  title="Howdah 1.2: guarding what PHP is about to do"
  description="Howdah 1.2 opens Layer 3 — sink hardening. It guards dangerous PHP sinks (command execution and unserialize on every supported PHP, include and eval on PHP 7.0+); it detects and logs, and does not block by default."
  path="/blog/howdah-1-2/"
>
  <article class="post">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> / <span aria-current="page">Howdah 1.2</span></p>
        <h1>Howdah 1.2: guarding what PHP is about to do</h1>
        <p class="lede"><time datetime="2026-10-24">24 October 2026</time></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>Howdah 1.2 is released. Layer 1 refuses abusive request <em>shapes</em>; Layer 2 reads the <em>content</em> of request values and logs what looks like an attack. Both watch the request on the way in. Layer 3 stands somewhere new: at the exact moment PHP is about to <em>do</em> something dangerous — run a shell command, unserialize attacker data, include a file, evaluate code — and checks it against a flat, operator-set policy. And, as with every Howdah layer, it watches first and tells you, before it ever gets in the way.</p>

      <div class="note" role="note">
        <p><strong>Update, 24 October 2026.</strong> Two things this post describes changed later. Howdah 1.3 made the <code>include</code> and <code>eval</code> guards active on PHP 5.x too, checked at compile time (see <a href="/blog/howdah-1-3/">Howdah 1.3</a>). And the 1.2.1 fix release closed a bypass of the multipart count rules in detect mode that affected every release up to 1.2.0.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>What's in 1.2: Layer 3, sink hardening</h2>
      <p>At a guarded <em>sink</em>, there is no guessing about whether something dangerous is happening — it is. That makes a decision there more defensible than an input signature. Howdah 1.2 guards four:</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><strong>Command / code execution</strong> — <code>system</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>shell_exec</code>, <code>passthru</code>, <code>popen</code>, <code>proc_open</code>. An anchored allowlist says which commands are fine; anything else is logged, or refused when you arm it.</li>
        <li><strong><code>unserialize</code></strong> — object-injection payloads. <strong>Off by default</strong>, because legitimate object unserialization is everywhere; you opt it in to hunt.</li>
        <li><strong><code>include</code> / <code>require</code></strong> and <strong><code>eval</code></strong> — on <strong>PHP 7.0+</strong>. Includes outside an allowed directory, and every <code>eval</code>, are flagged.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This is flat policy at the sink, in the lineage of Suhosin and Snuffleupagus — not taint tracking, which no tool can do honestly or completely. And it is <strong>sink hardening, not XSS</strong>: Layer 3 watches what the application <em>does</em>, not what it emits.</p>

      <h2>It logs. It does not block by default.</h2>
      <p>Layer 3 has its own mode, <code>sinks.mode</code>, which starts at <code>detect</code> (log only) and is <em>independent</em> of the global <code>howdah.mode</code> — so flipping <code>howdah.mode = block</code> does <strong>not</strong> quietly arm a sink. You read your logs, decide a sink is safe to enforce for your app, and arm that one explicitly (after setting its allowlist). Nobody upgrades to 1.2 and finds their site started refusing calls.</p>
      <p>When a sink <em>is</em> armed to block, Howdah does not take the request down mid-execution. It <strong>neutralizes the call</strong> the way PHP's own <code>disable_functions</code> does: the dangerous function returns <code>false</code> (or the include/eval is skipped) and the script keeps running. The operation simply never happens.</p>
      <p>And the matched value is <strong>never logged</strong>: an event records the sink and the function name (plus the class name for <code>unserialize</code>), never the command line, the include path, or the serialized payload. A flagged secret never ends up in your logs.</p>

      <h2>Honest about scope</h2>
      <p>Flat-policy virtual patching is best-effort and bypassable — an application can reach a dangerous effect by a route Howdah does not guard — which is <em>why</em> it logs first. A few honest edges, stated plainly: <code>include</code> and <code>eval</code> guards need <strong>PHP 7.0+</strong> (the 5.x opcode VM does not expose what we need; command execution and <code>unserialize</code> still work on 5.4+). <code>proc_open</code> with an array command, and any non-string first argument, are not inspected. And <strong><code>phar://</code> deserialization and SSRF are not in 1.2</strong> — they are the Layer-3 roadmap, and this site never claims a layer is here before it is.</p>
      <p>Every guard ships with a test that proves what it catches and what it leaves alone, across PHP 5.6, 7.4 and 8.3; the block path was reviewed internally for memory leaks before release (there has been no third-party audit). Our reproducers recreate only the harmless <em>shape</em> of an attack, never a working exploit.</p>

      <h2>Get it</h2>
      <p>If you are already on Howdah, <code>apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get upgrade</code> (or the yum equivalent) brings in 1.2; Layer 3 arrives in <code>detect</code> mode, logging only. New here? Start at the <a href="/install/">install picker</a>, read the <a href="/docs/configuration/#layer-3-sinks">configuration reference</a> for the <code>[sinks]</code> settings, and check the <a href="/coverage/">coverage table</a> and the per-version sink matrix for your PHP version. The source and this site are on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a> — if a sink flags something interesting on your traffic, we would like to hear about it.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

